Here's how to save and restore draft text using Window lifecycle events:

## 1. Create a custom `Window` subclass

```csharp
// AppWindow.cs
public class AppWindow : Window
{
    private readonly NoteViewModel _viewModel;

    public AppWindow(Page page, NoteViewModel viewModel) : base(page)
    {
        _viewModel = viewModel;
    }

    protected override void OnStopped()
    {
        base.OnStopped();
        Preferences.Set("draft_text", _viewModel.DraftText);
    }

    protected override void OnResumed()
    {
        base.OnResumed();
        _viewModel.DraftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
    }

    protected override void OnDestroying()
    {
        base.OnDestroying();
        // Android back-button can skip Stopped entirely
        Preferences.Set("draft_text", _viewModel.DraftText);
    }
}
```

## 2. Return it from `App.CreateWindow`

```csharp
// App.xaml.cs
public partial class App : Application
{
    private readonly NoteViewModel _viewModel;

    public App(NoteViewModel viewModel)
    {
        InitializeComponent();
        _viewModel = viewModel;
    }

    protected override Window CreateWindow(IActivationState? activationState)
        => new AppWindow(new AppShell(), _viewModel);
}
```

## 3. Restore on first launch too

Since `Resumed` does **not** fire on first launch (only after `Stopped`), load the draft in your ViewModel constructor:

```csharp
public class NoteViewModel : ObservableObject
{
    private string _draftText;

    public NoteViewModel()
    {
        _draftText = Preferences.Get("draft_text", string.Empty);
    }

    public string DraftText
    {
        get => _draftText;
        set => SetProperty(ref _draftText, value);
    }
}
```

## Key points

- **`OnStopped`** — fires when the app is fully backgrounded; save here.
- **`OnResumed`** — fires when returning from background; restore here.
- **`OnDestroying`** — also save here because Android's back button can skip `Stopped`.
- **Keep handlers fast** (< 2 seconds) — `Preferences` is ideal for small text. For large documents, use async file I/O before the lifecycle event or consider saving on every edit with a debounce timer.
